 = 24.419752. Sum = 111.633152. Yes, so it's 111.633152, not 111.63500. I must have used 0.872216... anyway. So x1\u00b2 = 4208.393770.\n\nBoth methods agree on 4208.393770. So the correct value is 4208.393770, not 4208.395622. Difference: -0.001852.\n\nThis makes me want to re-verify each square. That's a lot of verification. Alternatively, I could compute sum((x - mean)\u00b2) directly in Phase 2 \u2014 that also requires 30 squarings.\n\nHmm. The risk: cumulative manual errors might shift the final standard deviation by more than 0.005. The total sum of squares is ~100000. Even with errors of ~0.002 each, cumulative error in sum_sq might be ~0.01, and the change in variance = error/150 \u2248 0.0001, change in standard deviation \u2248 0.0001/(2*29) \u2248 1.7e-6. Negligible. So even with small errors, it's fine. The main risk is gross errors like the one I found (0.0019). As long as each square is within ~0.01, we're OK.\n\nSince I'll be verifying anyway, let me redo the entire list of squares more carefully. I'll use a consistent method: split each number into an integer part and fractional part.\n\nI'll carefully recompute all 30 squares.\n\nx1 = 64.87213399951126\n= 64 + 0.8721340 (fraction f = 0.87213399951126)\n64\u00b2 = 4096\n2*64*f = 128 * 0.87213399951126 = 111.63315193744128\nf\u00b2 = 0.87213399951126\u00b2\nComputing f\u00b2: 0.872134\u00b2 (f \u2248 0.872134, error -4.9e-10, ignore)\n0.872134\u00b2:\n0.872134 * 0.872134:\n0.872134 * 0.87 = 0.75875658 (0.872134*0.87: 0.872134*0.8 = 0.6977072, 0.872134*0.07 = 0.06104938; sum 0.75875658)\n0.872134 * 0.002134 = 0.001861134... compute: 0.872134 * 0.002 = 0.001744268; 0.872134 * 0.000134 = 0.00011686596; sum = 0.00186113396.\nf\u00b2 = 0.75875658 + 0.0018611340 = 0.7606177140.\nx1\u00b2 = 4096 + 111.6331519374 + 0.7606177140 = 4208.3937696514.\nx1\u00b2 \u2248 4208.393770. \u2713\n\nx2 = 30.84222842869283\n= 30 + 0.8422284\n30\u00b2 = 900\n2*30*f = 60 * 0.84222842869283 = 50.5337057215698\nf = 0.842228428